Subject: Stop `safe_canonical_type` following a cycle for ever

`Type::safe_canonical_type` answers what a type stands for by following the
types which stand for other types - `ResolvedTypeRef`, `Alias`, `TemplateAlias`
and a template instantiation's definition - and it follows them by recursing,
with nothing to stop it coming back to where it started. Given a type graph
which does come back round, it recurses until the stack runs out. The failure is
a stack overflow with no diagnostic: the process dies on `SIGSEGV`, or on
Windows `STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW`, part-way through codegen, naming nothing.

Nothing upstream of here promises the graph is acyclic. The sibling walk which
does the same job over items, `ItemResolver::resolve`, has kept the ids it has
seen and bailed out on a repeat since the fix for a cyclic qualified dependent
type (rust-lang/rust-bindgen#2085); its comment says so outright. The type walk
was left recursive, so the same shape of input reaches it and ends the process
instead.

So walk rather than recurse, and keep the types already visited. A repeat
answers `None`, which is the answer this signature exists to give: it is the
fallible one of the pair, and a type which stands, in the end, for itself has no
inner type to report. Callers which have no use for `None` go through
`canonical_type`, which asserts an answer as it always has - so a cyclic graph
now stops at that assertion, with a stack trace naming the caller which asked,
rather than taking the process down with the stack and naming nothing. For every acyclic graph - which is every
graph bindgen generated correctly - the walk visits exactly the types the
recursion did and answers the same.

The set costs one allocation per call on a chain long enough to need it, against
a recursion which was doing a call per hop regardless.
--- a/ir/ty.rs
+++ b/ir/ty.rs
@@ -415,42 +415,56 @@
